## sn3.25
sn3.25:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn3.25:1.2 | S: King Pasenadi of Kosala sat to one side, and the Buddha said to him, | T: Then King Pasenadi Kosala went to the Blessed One in the middle of the day and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him:
sn3.25:1.3 | S: “So, Great King, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?” | T: “Well now, great king, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?”
sn3.25:1.4 | S: “Sir, there are anointed aristocratic kings who are infatuated with authority, and obsessed with greed for sensual pleasures. They have attained stability in the country, occupying a vast conquered territory. Today I have been busy fulfilling the duties of such kings.” | T: “Just now, lord, I was engaged in the sort of royal affairs typical of head-anointed noble-warrior kings intoxicated with the intoxication of sovereignty, obsessed by greed for sensuality, who have attained stable control in their country, and who rule having conquered a great sphere of territory on earth.”
sn3.25:2.1 | S: “What do you think, Great King? | T: “What do you think, great king?
sn3.25:2.2 | S: Suppose a trustworthy and dependable man were to come from the east. | T: Suppose a trustworthy, reliable man were to come to you from the east.
sn3.25:2.3 | S: He’d approach you and say: | T: On arrival he would say to you,
sn3.25:2.4 | S: ‘Please, Great King, you should know this. I come from the east. | T: ‘May it please your majesty to know, I have come from the east.
sn3.25:2.5 | S: There I saw a big mountain that reached the clouds. And it was coming this way, crushing all creatures. | T: There I saw a great mountain, as high as the clouds, coming this way, crushing all living beings (in its path).
sn3.25:2.6 | S: So then, Great King, do what you must!’ | T: Do whatever you think should be done.’
sn3.25:2.7 | S: Then a second trustworthy and dependable man were to come from the west … | T: Then a second man were to come to you from the west…
sn3.25:2.8 | S: a third from the north … | T: Then a third man were to come to you from the north…
sn3.25:2.9 | S: and a fourth from the south. | T: Then a fourth trustworthy, reliable man were to come to you from the south.
sn3.25:2.10 | S: He’d approach you and say: | T: On arrival he would say to you,
sn3.25:2.11 | S: ‘Please, Great King, you should know this. I come from the south. | T: ‘May it please your majesty to know that I have come from the south.
sn3.25:2.12 | S: There I saw a big mountain that reached the clouds. And it was coming this way, crushing all creatures. | T: There I saw a great mountain, as high as the clouds, coming this way, crushing all living beings.
sn3.25:2.13 | S: So then, Great King, do what you must!’ | T: Do whatever you think should be done.’
sn3.25:2.14 | S: Should such a dire threat arise—a grim loss of human life, when human birth is so rare—what would you do?” | T: If, your majesty, such a great peril should arise, such a terrible destruction of human life—the human state being so hard to obtain—what should be done?”
sn3.25:3.1 | S: “Sir, what could I do but practice the teachings, practice morality, doing skillful and good actions?” | T: “If, lord, such a great peril should arise, such a terrible destruction of human life—the human state being so hard to obtain—what else should be done but Dhamma-conduct, right conduct, skillful deeds, meritorious deeds?”
sn3.25:4.1 | S: “I tell you, Great King, I announce to you: old age and death are advancing upon you. | T: “I inform you, great king, I announce to you, great king: aging & death are rolling in on you.
sn3.25:4.2 | S: Since old age and death are advancing upon you, what would you do?” | T: When aging & death are rolling in on you, what should be done?”
sn3.25:4.3 | S: “Sir, what can I do but practice the teachings, practice morality, doing skillful and good actions? | T: “As aging & death are rolling in on me, lord, what else should be done but Dhamma-conduct, right conduct, skillful deeds, meritorious deeds?
sn3.25:4.4 | S: Sir, there are anointed aristocratic kings who are infatuated with authority, and obsessed with greed for sensual pleasures. They have attained stability in the country, occupying a vast conquered territory. Such kings engage in battles of elephants, | T: “There are, lord, elephant battles (fought by) head-anointed noble-warrior kings intoxicated with the intoxication of sovereignty, obsessed by greed for sensuality, who have attained stable control in their country, and who rule having conquered a great sphere of territory on earth;
sn3.25:4.5 | P: tesampi, bhante, hatthiyuddhānaṁ natthi gati natthi visayo adhivattamāne jarāmaraṇe. | T: but there is no use for those elephant battles, no scope for them, when aging & death are rolling in.
sn3.25:4.6 | P: Yānipi tāni, bhante, raññaṁ khattiyānaṁ muddhāvasittānaṁ …pe… | T: (none)
sn3.25:4.7 | S: cavalry, | T: There are cavalry battles…
sn3.25:4.8 | S: chariots, | T: chariot battles…
sn3.25:4.9 | S: or infantry. | T: infantry battles…
sn3.25:4.10 | S: But there is no place, no domain for such battles when old age and death are advancing. | T: but there is no use for those infantry battles, no scope for them, when aging & death are rolling in.
sn3.25:4.11 | S: In this royal court there are ministers of wise counsel who are capable of dividing an approaching enemy by wise counsel. | T: In this royal court there are counselors who, when the enemies arrive, are capable of dividing them by their wits;
sn3.25:4.12 | S: But there is no place, no domain for such diplomatic battles when old age and death are advancing. | T: but there is no use for those battles of wits, no scope for them, when aging & death are rolling in.
sn3.25:4.13 | S: In this royal court there is abundant gold, both coined and uncoined, stored above and below ground. Using this wealth we can bribe an approaching enemy. | T: In this royal court there is abundant bullion & gold stored in vaults & depositories, and with such wealth we are capable of buying off enemies when they come;
sn3.25:4.14 | S: But there is no place, no domain for such monetary battles when old age and death are advancing. | T: but there is no use for those battles of wealth, no scope for them, when aging & death are rolling in.
sn3.25:4.15 | S: When old age and death are advancing, what can I do but practice the teachings, practice morality, doing skillful and good actions?” | T: As aging & death are rolling in on me, lord, what else should be done but Dhamma-conduct, right conduct, skillful deeds, meritorious deeds?”
sn3.25:5.1 | S: “That’s so true, Great King! That’s so true! | T: “So it is, great king! So it is, great king!
sn3.25:5.2 | S: When old age and death are advancing, what can you do but practice the teachings, practice morality, doing skillful and good actions?” | T: As aging & death are rolling in on you, what else should be done but Dhamma-conduct, right conduct, skillful deeds, meritorious deeds?”
sn3.25:5.3 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn3.25:5.4 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, further said this:
sn3.25:6.1 | S: “Suppose there were vast mountains | T: “Like massive boulders,
sn3.25:6.2 | S: of solid rock pressing against the sky | T: mountains pressing against the sky,
sn3.25:6.3 | S: drawing in from all sides | T: moving in from all sides,
sn3.25:6.4 | S: and crushing the four quarters. | T: crushing the four directions,
sn3.25:7.1 | S: So too old age and death | T: so aging & death
sn3.25:7.2 | S: advance upon all living creatures— | T: come rolling over living beings:
sn3.25:7.3 | S: aristocrats, brahmins, peasants, | T: noble warriors, brahmans, merchants,
sn3.25:7.4 | S: menials, corpse-workers, and scavengers. | T: workers, outcastes, & scavengers.
sn3.25:7.5 | S: They spare nothing. | T: They spare nothing.
sn3.25:7.6 | S: They crush all beneath them. | T: They trample everything.
sn3.25:8.1 | S: There’s nowhere for elephants to take a stand, | T: Here elephant troops can hold no ground,
sn3.25:8.2 | S: nor chariots nor infantry. | T: nor can chariots or infantry,
sn3.25:8.3 | S: They can’t be defeated | T: nor can a battle of wits
sn3.25:8.4 | S: by diplomatic battles or by wealth. | T: or wealth win out.
sn3.25:9.1 | S: That’s why an astute person, | T: So a wise person,
sn3.25:9.2 | S: seeing what’s good for themselves, | T: seeing his own good,
sn3.25:9.3 | S: being wise, would place faith | T: steadfast, secures confidence
sn3.25:9.4 | S: in the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha. | T: in the Buddha, Dhamma, & Saṅgha.
sn3.25:10.1 | S: Whoever lives by the teaching | T: One who practices the Dhamma
sn3.25:10.2 | S: in body, speech, and mind, | T: in thought, word, & deed,
sn3.25:10.3 | S: is praised in this life | T: receives praise here on earth
