## sn6.14
sn6.14:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
sn6.14:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling in Sāvatthī.
sn6.14:1.3 | S: There he addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
sn6.14:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
sn6.14:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
sn6.14:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
sn6.14:2.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, there was a king named Aruṇavā. | T: “In the past, monks, was a king named Aruṇavant
sn6.14:2.2 | S: He had a capital named Aruṇavatī. | T: whose royal city was named Aruṇavatī.
sn6.14:2.3 | S: Sikhī the Blessed One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha lived supported by Aruṇavatī. | T: And in dependence on that royal city of Aruṇavatī there dwelled Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened.
sn6.14:2.4 | S: Sikhī had a fine pair of chief disciples named Abhibhū and Sambhava. | T: And Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, had a pair of disciples named Abhibhū & Sambhava as his foremost pair, his excellent pair.
sn6.14:2.5 | S: Then the Buddha Sikhī addressed the mendicant Abhibhū, | T: “Then Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, addressed the monk Abhibhū:
sn6.14:2.6 | S: ‘Come, brahmin, let’s go to one of the realms of divinity until it’s time for our meal.’ | T: ‘Come, brahman, let’s go to a certain Brahmā world until the time for the meal.’
sn6.14:2.7 | S: ‘Yes, sir,’ replied Abhibhū. | T: “‘As you say, lord,’ the monk Abhibhū responded to Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened.
sn6.14:2.8 | S: Then, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, they vanished from Aruṇavatī and appeared in that realm of divinity. | T: “Then—just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, & the monk Abhibhū disappeared from the royal city of Aruṇavatī and reappeared in that Brahmā world.
sn6.14:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha Sikhī addressed the mendicant Abhibhū, | T: “Then Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, addressed the monk Abhibhū:
sn6.14:3.2 | S: ‘Brahmin, teach the Dhamma as you feel inspired for that divinity, his assembly, and the members of his retinue.’ | T: ‘Let a Dhamma talk come to you, brahman, for Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly.’
sn6.14:3.3 | S: ‘Yes, sir,’ replied Abhibhū. Then he educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired them with a Dhamma talk. | T: “Responding, ‘As you say, lord,’ to Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, the monk Abhibhū instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly with a Dhamma talk.
sn6.14:3.4 | S: But the Divinity, his assembly, and his retinue complained, grumbled, and objected, | T: “With that, Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly were indignant, annoyed, & complained:
sn6.14:3.5 | S: ‘Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! How on earth can a disciple teach Dhamma in the presence of the Teacher?’ | T: ‘Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding! How can this disciple, in the presence of his teacher, teach the Dhamma?’
sn6.14:4.1 | S: Then the Buddha Sikhī addressed the mendicant Abhibhū, | T: “Then Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, addressed the monk Abhibhū:
sn6.14:4.2 | S: ‘Brahmin, the Divinity, his assembly, and his retinue are complaining | T: ‘Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly are indignant & annoyed with you. They complain:
sn6.14:4.3 | S: that a disciple teaches Dhamma in the presence of the Teacher. | T: “Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding! How can this disciple, in the presence of his teacher, teach the Dhamma?”
sn6.14:4.4 | S: Well then, brahmin, stir them up even more!’ | T: Very well then, strike terror in Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly to an even greater degree.’
sn6.14:4.5 | S: ‘Yes, sir,’ replied Abhibhū. Then he taught Dhamma with his body visible; with his body invisible; with the lower half visible and the upper half invisible; and with the upper half visible and the lower half invisible. | T: “Responding, ‘As you say, lord,’ to Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, the monk Abhibhū taught the Dhamma with his body visible, taught the Dhamma with his body invisible, taught the Dhamma with the lower half of his body visible & the upper half invisible, taught the Dhamma with the upper half of his body visible & the lower half invisible.
sn6.14:4.6 | S: And the Divinity, his assembly, and his retinue, their minds full of wonder and amazement, thought, | T: “With that, the minds of Brahmā, Brahmā’s retinue, & Brahmā’s assembly were overcome with amazement & astonishment:
sn6.14:4.7 | S: ‘Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! The ascetic has such psychic power and might!’ | T: ‘Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding!—the contemplative’s great power & great might!’
sn6.14:5.1 | S: Then Abhibhū said to the Buddha Sikhī, | T: “Then the monk Abhibhū said to Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened,
sn6.14:5.2 | S: ‘Sir, I recall having said this in the middle of the Saṅgha: | T: ‘I recall, lord, having made this statement while standing in the midst of the Saṅgha of monks:
sn6.14:5.3 | S: “Standing in the realm of divinity, I can make my voice heard throughout the thousandfold galaxy.”’ | T: “I can, friends, while standing in the Brahmā world, make my voice heard throughout the thousand-fold cosmos.”’
sn6.14:5.4 | S: ‘Now is the time, brahmin! Now is the time, brahmin! | T: “‘This is the time, brahman! This is the time, brahman,
sn6.14:5.5 | S: Standing in the realm of divinity, make your voice heard throughout the thousandfold galaxy.’ | T: for you, while standing in the Brahmā world, to make your voice heard throughout the thousand-fold cosmos!’
sn6.14:5.6 | S: ‘Yes, sir,’ replied Abhibhū. Standing in the realm of divinity, he spoke this verse: | T: “Responding, ‘As you say, lord,’ to Sikhī the Blessed One, worthy & fully self-awakened, the monk Abhibhū, while standing in the Brahmā world, recited these verses:
sn6.14:6.1 | S: ‘Rouse yourselves! Try harder! | T: ‘Rouse yourselves. Go forth.
sn6.14:6.2 | S: Devote yourselves to the teachings of the Buddha! | T: Commit yourselves to the Awakened One’s bidding.
sn6.14:6.3 | S: Crush the army of Death, | T: Scatter the army of Death
sn6.14:6.4 | S: as an elephant a hut of reeds. | T: as an elephant would a shed made of reeds.
sn6.14:7.1 | S: Whoever shall meditate diligently | T: He who,
sn6.14:7.2 | S: in this teaching and training, | T: in this Dhamma & Vinaya, stays heedful,
sn6.14:7.3 | S: giving up transmigration through rebirths, | T: abandoning birth, the wandering-on,
