## sn55.26
sn55.26:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Sāvatthī.
sn55.26:1.2 | S: Now at that time the householder Anāthapiṇḍika was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: Now, on that occasion Anāthapiṇḍika the householder was diseased, in pain, severely ill.
sn55.26:1.3 | S: Then he addressed a man, | T: Then he said to one of his men,
sn55.26:1.4 | S: “Please, my man, go to Venerable Sāriputta, and in my name bow with your head to his feet. Say to him: | T: “Come, my good man. Go to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, pay homage to his feet with your head in my name and say
sn55.26:1.5 | S: ‘Sir, the householder Anāthapiṇḍika is sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: ‘Venerable sir, Anāthapiṇḍika the householder is diseased, in pain, severely ill.
sn55.26:1.6 | S: He bows with his head to your feet.’ | T: He pays homage with his head to your feet.’
sn55.26:1.7 | S: And then say: | T: Then say:
sn55.26:1.8 | S: ‘Sir, please visit him at his home out of sympathy.’” | T: ‘It would be good if Ven. Sāriputta would visit Anāthapiṇḍika’s home, out of sympathy for him.’”
sn55.26:2.1 | S: “Yes, sir,” that man replied. He did as Anāthapiṇḍika asked. | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to Anāthapiṇḍika the householder, the man went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said,
sn55.26:3.1 | P: “Anāthapiṇḍiko, bhante, gahapati ābādhiko dukkhito bāḷhagilāno. | T: “Venerable sir, Anāthapiṇḍika the householder is diseased, in pain, severely ill.
sn55.26:3.2 | P: So āyasmato sāriputtassa pāde sirasā vandati. | T: He pays homage with his head to your feet.”
sn55.26:3.3 | P: Evañca vadati: | T: Then he said,
sn55.26:3.4 | P: ‘sādhu kira, bhante, āyasmā sāriputto yena anāthapiṇḍikassa gahapatissa nivesanaṁ tenupasaṅkamatu anukampaṁ upādāyā’”ti. | T: “It would be good if Ven. Sāriputta would visit Anāthapiṇḍika’s home, out of sympathy for him.”
sn55.26:3.5 | S: Sāriputta consented with silence. | T: Ven. Sāriputta acquiesced with silence.
sn55.26:4.1 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went with Venerable Ānanda as his second monk to Anāthapiṇḍika’s home. He sat down on the seat spread out and said to Anāthapiṇḍika, | T: Then early in the morning, Ven. Sāriputta—having adjusted his under robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe—went to Anāthapiṇḍika’s home with Ven. Ānanda as his companion. On arrival, he sat down on a seat made ready. Seated, he said to Anāthapiṇḍika:
sn55.26:4.2 | S: “I hope you’re keeping well, householder; I hope you’re all right. I hope your pain is fading, not growing; that its fading is evident, not its growing.” | T: “I hope you are getting better, householder. I hope you are comfortable. I hope that your pains are lessening and not increasing. I hope that there are signs of their lessening, and not of their increasing.”
sn55.26:4.3 | S: “Sir, I’m not keeping well, I’m not getting by. My pain is terrible and growing, not fading; its growing is evident, not its fading.” | T: “I am not getting better, venerable sir. I am not comfortable. My extreme pains are increasing, not lessening. There are signs of their increasing, and not of their lessening.”
sn55.26:5.1 | S: “Householder, you don’t have the distrust in the Buddha that causes an unlearned ordinary person to be reborn—when their body breaks up, after death—in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of suspicion of the Buddha endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:5.2 | S: And you have experiential confidence in the Buddha: | T: You have verified confidence in the Buddha:
sn55.26:5.3 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
sn55.26:5.4 | S: Seeing in yourself that experiential confidence in the Buddha, that pain may die down on the spot. | T: As you see that verified confidence in the Buddha in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.
sn55.26:6.1 | S: You don’t have the distrust in the teaching that causes an unlearned ordinary person to be reborn—when their body breaks up, after death—in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of suspicion of the Dhamma endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:6.2 | S: And you have experiential confidence in the teaching: | T: You have verified confidence in the Dhamma:
sn55.26:6.3 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.’ | T: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
sn55.26:6.4 | S: Seeing in yourself that experiential confidence in the teaching, that pain may die down on the spot. | T: As you see that verified confidence in the Dhamma in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.
sn55.26:7.1 | S: You don’t have the distrust in the Saṅgha that causes an unlearned ordinary person to be reborn—when their body breaks up, after death—in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of suspicion of the Saṅgha endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:7.2 | S: And you have experiential confidence in the Saṅgha: | T: You have verified confidence in the Saṅgha:
sn55.26:7.3 | S: ‘The Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is practicing the way that’s good, direct, systematic, and proper. It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, and worthy of veneration with cupped palms. It is the supreme field of merit for the world.’ | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… who have practiced straight-forwardly… who have practiced methodically… who have practiced masterfully—in other words, the four types of noble disciples when taken as pairs, the eight when taken as individual types—they are the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples: worthy of gifts, worthy of hospitality, worthy of offerings, worthy of respect, the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
sn55.26:7.4 | S: Seeing in yourself that experiential confidence in the Saṅgha, that pain may die down on the spot. | T: As you see that verified confidence in the Saṅgha in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.
sn55.26:8.1 | S: You don’t have the unethical conduct that causes an unlearned ordinary person to be reborn—when their body breaks up, after death—in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of poor virtue endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:8.2 | S: Your ethical conduct is loved by the noble ones, unbroken, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. | T: You have virtues appealing to the noble ones: untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, leading to concentration.
sn55.26:8.3 | S: Seeing in yourself that ethical conduct loved by the noble ones, that pain may die down on the spot. | T: As you see those virtues appealing to the noble ones in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.
sn55.26:9.1 | S: You don’t have the wrong view that causes an unlearned ordinary person to be reborn—when their body breaks up, after death—in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of wrong view endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:9.2 | S: You have right view. | T: You have right view.
sn55.26:9.3 | S: Seeing in yourself that right view, that pain may die down on the spot. | T: As you see that right view in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.
sn55.26:10.1 | S: You don’t have the wrong purpose … | T: “Householder, you don’t have the type of wrong resolve…
sn55.26:10.2 | P: Atthi ca kho te, gahapati, sammāsaṅkappo. | T: (none)
