sn55.26:18.1 | S: wrong freedom … | T: wrong release endowed with which an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—with the breakup of the body, after death—re-appears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.
sn55.26:18.2 | S: You have right freedom. | T: You have right release.
sn55.26:18.3 | S: Seeing in yourself that right freedom, that pain may die down on the spot.” | T: As you see that right release in yourself, your pains may immediately grow calm.”
sn55.26:19.1 | S: And then Anāthapiṇḍika’s pain died down on the spot. | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika’s pains immediately grew calm.
sn55.26:19.2 | S: Then he served Sāriputta and Ānanda from his own dish. | T: So he served Ven. Sāriputta & Ven. Ānanda from his very own dish.
sn55.26:19.3 | S: When Sāriputta had eaten and washed his hand and bowl, Anāthapiṇḍika took a low seat and sat to one side. | T: Then, when Ven. Sāriputta had finished his meal and had rinsed his bowl & hands, Anāthapiṇḍika the householder, taking a low seat, sat to one side.
sn55.26:19.4 | S: Venerable Sāriputta expressed his appreciation to him with these verses. | T: As he was sitting there, Ven. Sāriputta gave his approval with these verses:
sn55.26:20.1 | S: “Whoever has faith in the Realized One, | T: One whose conviction in the Tathāgata
sn55.26:20.2 | S: unwavering and well grounded; | T: is well-established, unshakable;
sn55.26:20.3 | S: whose ethical conduct is good, | T: whose virtue is admirable,
sn55.26:20.4 | S: praised and loved by the noble ones; | T: appealing to the noble ones, praised;
sn55.26:21.1 | S: who has confidence in the Saṅgha, | T: who has confidence in the Saṅgha,
sn55.26:21.2 | S: and correct view: | T: & vision made straight:
sn55.26:21.3 | S: they’re said to be prosperous, | T: “Not poor,” they say of him.
sn55.26:21.4 | S: their life is not in vain. | T: Not in vain his life.
sn55.26:22.1 | S: So let the wise devote themselves | T: (none)
sn55.26:22.2 | S: to faith, ethical behaviour, | T: So conviction & virtue,
sn55.26:22.3 | S: confidence, and insight into the teaching, | T: confidence & Dhamma-vision should be cultivated by the intelligent,
sn55.26:22.4 | S: remembering the instructions of the Buddhas.” | T: remembering the Buddhas’ teachings.
sn55.26:23.1 | S: After expressing his appreciation to Anāthapiṇḍika with these verses, Sāriputta rose from his seat and left. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, having given his approval to Anāthapiṇḍika the householder, got up from his seat and left.
sn55.26:24.1 | S: Then Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
sn55.26:24.2 | S: “So, Ānanda, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?” | T: “Well, now, Ānanda, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?”
sn55.26:24.3 | S: “Sir, Venerable Sāriputta advised the householder Anāthapiṇḍika in this way and that.” | T: “Lord, Anāthapiṇḍika the householder was instructed by Ven. Sāriputta with this & this instruction.”
sn55.26:24.4 | S: “Sāriputta is astute, Ānanda. | T: “Sāriputta is wise, Ānanda,
sn55.26:24.5 | S: He has great wisdom, since he can analyze the four factors of stream-entry in ten respects.” | T: & greatly discerning, in that he analyzes the four factors of stream entry into ten implications.”
sn55.26:24.6 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)


